\f0\fs24 \cf0 Note that the firewall daemon, firewalld, on Manjaro, blocks multicast; something on OpenBSD is interfering with mDNS but not multicast, since logging works.\
\
If Avahi can't be made to work on OpenBSD, consider using multicast to manage the Registry announcements - a service looking to the Registry can send a multicast request, waiting on a UDP port for a response and timing out if no response is received.\
Avahi sort-of works, except it seems to stop refreshing its cache - it doesn't show all the services on the same machine, let alone all the services on the network.\
\
Rough logic flow, non-Registry service or utility:\
1. Send a request to the Registry multicast port, asking for the TCP/IP connection information.\
2. Wait for the number of seconds of a retry interval.\
3. If there is no response within the retry interval and there are retry intervals left, go back to the first step.\
4. If there is a response, record the connection details.\
5. Close the Registry multicast port.\
6. Exit if there was no response.\
7. Proceed with normal activity.\
\
Rough logic flow, Registry service:\
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 1. Send a request to the Registry multicast port, asking for the TCP/IP connection information.\
2. Wait for the number of seconds of a retry interval.\
3. If there is no response within the retry interval and there are retry intervals left, go back to the first step.\
4. If there is a response, report the connection details and exit.\
5. Start the Registry TCP/IP listener.\
6. If a request arrives on the Registry multicast port, respond with the details of the Registry TCP/IP listener.\
7. If a request arrives on the request port, process it and go back to the previous step unless the request is to stop.\
\
Note that this is similarly structured to the mDNS code, but is simpler since very little needs to be transferred.\
The multicast code already exists as part of nImOlog and nImOpost,\
\
Note that ContextWithMDNS is the parent of ServiceContext and UtilityContext and is widely referenced; the initial code should be parallel to the mDNS support - the non-Registry utilities or services can watch for the Registry, while the Registry can 'announce' itself when queried, but not advance to handling requests until this has been tested.\
